--TEST--
A call through a `callable` that names its parameters answers the declared return type and converts each argument where it is written, while bare `callable` still answers `mixed` — `rule:types/callable-signature`, `rule:types/callable-arity`, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The signature names both parameters and the return type, so the call is
// checked here: the result needs no conversion to reach a `string` binding.
callable(int, string): string $join = fn (int $n, string $s): string => $s . $n;
string $joined = $join(2, "x");
echo $joined, "\n";

// `rule:types/callable-arity`: what the value holds may declare fewer
// parameters than the type names, and the runtime hands that callable only the
// ones it declares — so the call site still passes what the *type* names.
callable(int): int $seven = fn (): int => 7;
echo $seven(1), "\n";

// `rule:types/conversion`'s one implicit widening, at the site that stopped
// asking the runtime for it: an `int` argument into a `float` parameter.
callable(float): float $half = fn (float $f): float => $f / 2.0;
echo $half(7), "\n";

// Bare `callable` is the top of the lattice and names no parameter, so the
// call answers `mixed` and the conversion is written.
callable $bare = fn (int $n): int => $n + 1;
echo $bare(2) as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
x2
7
3.5
3
